# The preset policy in kgsm-base enables the SOCKET and disables the service. The service is meant
# to sit inactive: it wakes on the first connection and exits again after Speech__IdleMinutes, which
# is the only thing that returns the ~1.6GB and ~1.1GB of video memory the models cost.
#
# Applying the policy is symlink manipulation on disk, so it works during the transaction and in a
# chroot alike. Arming the socket is the node's post-transaction hook's job.
#
# post_install only. An administrator who disabled the socket keeps it disabled across every later
# version — re-presetting on upgrade would quietly undo that.

post_install() {
    systemctl preset kgsm-speech.socket kgsm-speech.service >/dev/null 2>&1 || true

    cat <<'MSG'

  kgsm-speech is enabled and can hear and speak. The models arrived with it in kgsm-speech-models
  and sit in /var/lib/kgsm-speech/models, where they survive every upgrade.

  Check it with:  kgsm-speech --voices     (activates the daemon; loads no model)

  Run that as root or as a member of the kgsm group — the socket is 0660 kgsm:kgsm, and those
  permissions are this daemon's whole access boundary.

  An inactive kgsm-speech.service is this leaf working correctly. Optional overrides live in
  /etc/kgsm-speech/kgsm-speech.env; without a usable GPU both recognition and synthesis fall back to
  the processor rather than refusing.

  kgsm-speech-fetch-models is the repair and relocation tool: it verifies the models, replaces one
  that does not match its digest, and fetches into a MODEL_DIR of your choosing for a host that
  keeps 813MB somewhere other than /var/lib.

MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
    cat <<'MSG'

  The daemon holds the old binary until it exits, so the node brings the socket and the service down
  together and re-arms the socket. Nothing here needs doing by hand.

MSG
}
